1
00:00:01,992 --> 00:00:21,807
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, based on our yesterday's discussion, I was thinking that, peripherally by talking about, can it be understood as the field on which the particular manifests?

2
00:00:22,267 --> 00:00:25,290
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes and no.

3
00:00:25,550 --> 00:00:27,672
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, in a sense that, yeah, it is.

4
00:00:28,072 --> 00:00:30,554
[SPEAKER_01]: A particular, they arise on the basis

5
00:00:31,586 --> 00:00:35,788
[SPEAKER_01]: of that more general or more peripheral.

6
00:00:35,828 --> 00:00:48,994
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the sense bases, for example, are referred to as... can be translated as fields, with the same... in the same sense, actually, because the sense objects that arise on a particular level, they arise on the basis of the field of the eye.

7
00:00:50,075 --> 00:00:52,176
[SPEAKER_01]: But the problem is, eh...

8
00:00:53,686 --> 00:01:05,962
[SPEAKER_01]: I said that that's why it's yes, but it can be answered no, if you give that field some sort of priority over the particulars, because that field is a negative phenomena.

9
00:01:06,323 --> 00:01:10,228
[SPEAKER_01]: It's defined by the things that arise particularly.

10
00:01:11,303 --> 00:01:16,965
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can discern that there is a field because the I-objects are present.

11
00:01:17,645 --> 00:01:23,487
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the particular things have a reason, the peripheral is generated because of that.

12
00:01:23,928 --> 00:01:25,328
[SPEAKER_01]: It's defined because of that.

13
00:01:25,788 --> 00:01:28,169
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not independent of the particular.

14
00:01:28,929 --> 00:01:37,492
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I mean, the question you ask is valid, but sometimes it can be asked in a sense of which is first, which is more important, which is greater.

15
00:01:39,393 --> 00:01:47,121
[SPEAKER_01]: And, in that sense, I have to be careful how to answer, because then people will just think, ah, okay, so there's this field, and then things arise upon the field, but...

16
00:01:47,921 --> 00:01:56,610
[SPEAKER_01]: If you think like that, you're taking that field to be the particular, which means something else is then peripheral in regard to that thought of that very defined field.

17
00:01:57,946 --> 00:02:04,071
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's one thing to understand the level of your mind, sort of thinking, intellectually.

18
00:02:04,091 --> 00:02:04,311
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

19
00:02:04,411 --> 00:02:06,633
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's important to clarify as much as you can.

20
00:02:07,133 --> 00:02:14,780
[SPEAKER_01]: But, as I said, if you want to recognize it as the actual phenomena that which that field is, that is always generated on the basis of the current particulars.

21
00:02:15,625 --> 00:02:16,426
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how you start.

22
00:02:16,526 --> 00:02:19,729
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you're just mindful of the present situation of the body and so on.

23
00:02:19,829 --> 00:02:22,712
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, based on that, this is already there.

24
00:02:22,812 --> 00:02:24,013
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you just need to discern it.

25
00:02:24,453 --> 00:02:28,577
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, recognize that which you have intellectually grasped beforehand.

26
00:02:29,398 --> 00:02:36,725
[SPEAKER_01]: Because now you have, sort of, the information and the data to... to, sort of, eh... identify.

27
00:02:37,650 --> 00:02:44,313
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what you have, nāmarūpa determines viññāṇa in as much as viññāṇa determines nāmarūpa.

28
00:02:44,333 --> 00:02:46,094
[SPEAKER_01]: Two sticks holding each other.

29
00:02:46,134 --> 00:02:47,515
[SPEAKER_01]: One is not more than the other.

30
00:02:47,575 --> 00:02:51,817
[SPEAKER_01]: And one cannot even be conceived without the other being simultaneously present.

31
00:03:03,890 --> 00:03:13,903
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how, by full understanding that, there is absolutely no room for the sense of self, for the appropriation to remain there, because it becomes completely redundant.

32
00:03:14,824 --> 00:03:15,665
[SPEAKER_01]: The whole picture...

33
00:03:16,467 --> 00:03:19,750
[SPEAKER_01]: These two sticks, they don't need any master operator.

34
00:03:20,310 --> 00:03:22,152
[SPEAKER_01]: Basically, they support each other.

35
00:03:22,732 --> 00:03:31,019
[SPEAKER_01]: For as long as the five aggregates are present, things will be arising and manifesting and defining itself, defining the... and everything else will continue operating, so to speak.

36
00:03:31,579 --> 00:03:33,461
[SPEAKER_01]: The intention, the volition, all that would stay.

37
00:03:34,001 --> 00:03:36,563
[SPEAKER_01]: But what wouldn't stay is the owner of it.

38
00:03:36,764 --> 00:03:38,025
[SPEAKER_01]: Because there's no room for the owner.

39
00:03:38,065 --> 00:03:39,226
[SPEAKER_01]: The owner is part of that stick.

40
00:03:39,246 --> 00:03:40,086
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what can you own?

41
00:03:40,647 --> 00:03:41,647
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you own the left stick?

42
00:03:42,510 --> 00:03:44,591
[SPEAKER_01]: If it's being supported by the right one, you can't.

43
00:03:44,651 --> 00:03:46,551
[SPEAKER_01]: You realize, well, I might think I own it.

44
00:03:46,611 --> 00:03:48,992
[SPEAKER_01]: If the right stick goes, the left one cannot stay.

45
00:03:49,352 --> 00:03:52,013
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you own the right stick, if it's being held by the left one.

46
00:03:52,193 --> 00:04:00,415
[SPEAKER_01]: And you can't hold them both at the same time, because that will imply you stepping outside of your experience and then taking up the hold of its foundations.

47
00:04:00,455 --> 00:04:01,296
[SPEAKER_01]: But you can't do that.

48
00:04:01,756 --> 00:04:04,277
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's the practice, basically.

49
00:04:04,297 --> 00:04:05,877
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the vipassana, in that sense.

50
00:04:06,097 --> 00:04:08,218
[SPEAKER_01]: It's that repetition or that contemplation

51
00:04:08,898 --> 00:04:21,968
[SPEAKER_01]: of this, uh, mutual dependence of these things whereby, by fully discerning that, you automatically squeeze out any sense of appropriation itself, because it's just inconceivable to be there at the same time.

52
00:04:22,829 --> 00:04:23,029
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

53
00:04:24,050 --> 00:04:25,951
[SPEAKER_01]: It becomes redundant, completely.

54
00:04:33,277 --> 00:04:33,537
[SPEAKER_01]: So,

55
00:04:35,392 --> 00:04:39,154
[SPEAKER_01]: If you use the approach of saḷāyātana, the sense bases, yes, they're the negative.

56
00:04:39,614 --> 00:04:40,254
[SPEAKER_01]: They're the base.

57
00:04:40,514 --> 00:04:41,174
[SPEAKER_01]: They're the field.

58
00:04:42,135 --> 00:04:47,797
[SPEAKER_01]: And I think the old translations of Jnana-mūlitera, the first, was the...

59
00:04:47,817 --> 00:04:49,537
[SPEAKER_01]: He was certainly experimenting with it at some point.

60
00:04:49,757 --> 00:04:50,578
[SPEAKER_01]: It was a field.

61
00:04:50,958 --> 00:04:51,338
[SPEAKER_01]: A field.

62
00:04:51,458 --> 00:04:53,499
[SPEAKER_01]: I think Navid was as well referring to it as such.

63
00:04:53,939 --> 00:04:54,199
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

64
00:04:55,360 --> 00:05:01,622
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, because, like, from yesterday, when I was meditating,

65
00:05:02,764 --> 00:05:26,672
[SPEAKER_00]: I actually realized that it seems that all these saḷāyātanas are all the time operating, and your attention just picked up one small piece, one moment, from that whole, you know, inconceivable amount of possibilities, and you actually have no idea what will come in the next

66
00:05:27,608 --> 00:05:34,450
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, you have a very limited idea, and that limit is given to you by the thing beforehand.

67
00:05:34,470 --> 00:05:37,610
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in that sense, you're not in control even of that idea.

68
00:05:38,210 --> 00:05:41,271
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you just come, like, afterwards.

69
00:05:41,331 --> 00:05:44,692
[SPEAKER_00]: Everything is already set up, and you just come on the scene.

70
00:05:44,832 --> 00:05:45,252
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

71
00:05:52,853 --> 00:05:53,234
[SPEAKER_01]: And it is.

72
00:05:53,274 --> 00:05:54,574
[SPEAKER_01]: The six sense bases

73
00:05:55,509 --> 00:06:04,693
[SPEAKER_01]: These six fields of possibilities, these six negatives, six peripherals, if that's the approach you take, eh... yeah, they're simultaneously present there all the time.

74
00:06:06,654 --> 00:06:09,795
[SPEAKER_01]: And your attention obviously arises on the basis of one particular among them.

75
00:06:10,615 --> 00:06:15,677
[SPEAKER_01]: Veda, I thought, whatever takes... takes your fancy or presses you the hardest.

76
00:06:15,697 --> 00:06:15,978
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

77
00:06:16,498 --> 00:06:19,279
[SPEAKER_01]: And can you explain what keeps the negative

78
00:06:20,788 --> 00:06:28,030
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, the narrative is kept by the mind, because the five senses meet in the mind.

79
00:06:28,550 --> 00:06:34,552
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the mind does, for its own object, has the overview of the five senses.

80
00:06:35,212 --> 00:06:36,492
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how they are intelligible.

81
00:06:36,852 --> 00:06:37,092
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

82
00:06:37,773 --> 00:06:42,554
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, you have a... Putujana has an extra layer of narrative, which is his ātrāda.

83
00:06:44,200 --> 00:06:45,101
[SPEAKER_01]: And his sense of self.

84
00:06:45,501 --> 00:06:51,404
[SPEAKER_01]: That adds a whole another assumed dimension of another point of view that's external to all of this.

85
00:06:52,645 --> 00:06:54,626
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, the mind, that's just arahant as well.

86
00:06:54,666 --> 00:07:04,752
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, he he has a narrative, he has a context, he he can still operate and talk and discern and choose and prefer, because the mind is where the senses meet.

87
00:07:05,313 --> 00:07:11,597
[SPEAKER_01]: Obviously, the mind cannot be overwhelmed by the senses anymore, so nothing that he chooses or prefers will be based on anything unwholesome.

88
00:07:13,077 --> 00:07:22,542
[SPEAKER_01]: But for Putrajana, he has assumed another completely gratuitous dimension of his sense of self being independent of both that mind and all those senses.

89
00:07:22,622 --> 00:07:24,423
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just completely outside.

90
00:07:24,503 --> 00:07:25,924
[SPEAKER_01]: It's holding both of the sticks.

91
00:07:26,344 --> 00:07:27,664
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's the idea of the self.

92
00:07:27,745 --> 00:07:29,305
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the notion of self.

93
00:07:29,565 --> 00:07:30,566
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the meaning of it.

94
00:07:30,666 --> 00:07:31,386
[SPEAKER_01]: The mastery.

95
00:07:31,686 --> 00:07:32,587
[SPEAKER_01]: You are in charge.

96
00:07:33,067 --> 00:07:33,207
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

97
00:07:33,487 --> 00:07:37,749
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can see how much more complex than the narrative becomes.

98
00:07:37,769 --> 00:07:39,290
[SPEAKER_01]: And much more unpredictable as well.

99
00:07:40,130 --> 00:07:40,791
[SPEAKER_01]: And, eh...

100
00:07:41,687 --> 00:07:56,755
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's exactly... that's exactly why, like, having the sense of self results in suffering, results... because you're trying... you're sort of acting as if you are the center of experience, yet, like, every single experience you have, that is being undermined through the very experience.

101
00:07:57,765 --> 00:08:16,123
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's why if a patujana would stop engaging in the world, stop engaging in the desires, practicing restraint, go and close himself in a little cell, in a little room, first it would be the boredom that would come, and then it would be a sheer dread and fear, and many people would lose their minds.

102
00:08:16,776 --> 00:08:19,798
[SPEAKER_01]: If there is nothing, nothing taking away that... Why?

103
00:08:19,838 --> 00:08:21,079
[SPEAKER_01]: Because they remain holding.

104
00:08:22,200 --> 00:08:32,788
[SPEAKER_01]: They cannot not hold to their sense of self, to that assumed external entity, really, that is just being now crushed from every possible conceivable direction.

105
00:08:33,620 --> 00:08:35,581
[SPEAKER_01]: And he recognizes the prospect of it.

106
00:08:35,601 --> 00:08:36,221
[SPEAKER_01]: He feels it.

107
00:08:36,961 --> 00:08:38,101
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it's so terrifying.

108
00:08:38,462 --> 00:08:52,306
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why when the quotidian would talk to the Buddha sometimes, and he would tell what he teaches, they would understand what he... they would understand the dispassion, the point, the view of nibbāna, like abandoning it all before it's taken away.

109
00:08:53,540 --> 00:08:55,381
[SPEAKER_01]: But they wouldn't let go of their sense of self.

110
00:08:56,002 --> 00:08:58,823
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, that understanding would completely make them go mad.

111
00:08:58,863 --> 00:09:01,245
[SPEAKER_01]: They would fall on the ground, pull their hair, beat their breast.

112
00:09:01,825 --> 00:09:04,647
[SPEAKER_01]: Basically, full-on panic attack and dread.

113
00:09:04,727 --> 00:09:06,348
[SPEAKER_01]: Because they don't let go of the self.

114
00:09:07,128 --> 00:09:12,492
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, they understood clearly that all these things, they come to an end, that self owns.

115
00:09:12,772 --> 00:09:12,992
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet,

116
00:09:14,016 --> 00:09:18,841
[SPEAKER_01]: If the self owns, means self will be owning its ending as well, the ending of those things, but it can't.

117
00:09:19,281 --> 00:09:23,985
[SPEAKER_01]: So you realize the self will be destroyed, and then they would lament and say, well, that will destroy us, that will kill us.

118
00:09:24,466 --> 00:09:25,567
[SPEAKER_01]: There will be nothing left.

119
00:09:26,788 --> 00:09:30,752
[SPEAKER_01]: Because in their mind it's the self that sort of governs all of these things.

120
00:09:43,184 --> 00:10:02,775
[SPEAKER_00]: It seems that this approach can really disentangle that notion of start, because if you realize how broad everything is, that it's not just your particular experience, but there is that whole thing around, which you can't control.

121
00:10:02,855 --> 00:10:04,956
[SPEAKER_01]: Probably it's a bad word.

122
00:10:05,056 --> 00:10:08,078
[SPEAKER_01]: I know what you mean, but it probably means it will extinguish.

123
00:10:10,399 --> 00:10:21,446
[SPEAKER_01]: That dimension that is being gratuitously maintained and generated as the external dimension to the whole experience, which is where the self abides and lives as the master, as the god.

124
00:10:22,555 --> 00:10:29,257
[SPEAKER_01]: Once you start noticing that that dimension, it's already peripheral, that has peripheral to it, you realize there is no outside to you.

125
00:10:30,197 --> 00:10:33,278
[SPEAKER_01]: Because when you think outside, that's the experience inside.

126
00:10:34,439 --> 00:10:38,000
[SPEAKER_01]: So even your thoughts of external are experienced right here, right now, internally.

127
00:10:38,400 --> 00:10:40,680
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means you can't step outside of your experience.

128
00:10:41,701 --> 00:10:44,281
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's not hard to grasp on the intellectual level.

129
00:10:45,002 --> 00:10:46,002
[SPEAKER_01]: It kind of makes sense.

130
00:10:47,487 --> 00:10:58,933
[SPEAKER_01]: Willing it long enough until the mind stops, sort of, wavering and going out of it, even with those kind of careless, lazy, sort of, thinking assumptions, that will take time.

131
00:10:59,653 --> 00:11:08,938
[SPEAKER_01]: But the more you do it, the more, sort of, you're kind of, well, as I said, you're sort of fading away.

132
00:11:09,559 --> 00:11:24,699
[SPEAKER_01]: That notion of external dimension, because no matter what external dimension you're thinking presently, right now, mindfully, you realize that's already surrounded by its own peripheral, which means it cannot be the ultimate external, which means it cannot be the ultimate owner, the ultimate self, the ultimate God, the ultimate anywhere.

133
00:11:25,219 --> 00:11:27,883
[SPEAKER_01]: It's all a reason on the basis of the particular experience.

134
00:11:29,432 --> 00:11:30,993
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you realize, yeah, that was the whole point.

135
00:11:31,013 --> 00:11:40,318
[SPEAKER_01]: It was just that little kind of careless, careless way of attending to things, assuming the external to yourself, to your experience, to others, doesn't matter.

136
00:11:40,738 --> 00:11:41,719
[SPEAKER_01]: Just that was enough.

137
00:11:41,839 --> 00:11:43,740
[SPEAKER_01]: That was enough for this whole mess of saṃsāra.

138
00:11:44,000 --> 00:11:44,460
[SPEAKER_01]: Nothing else.

139
00:11:45,241 --> 00:11:48,282
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why ignorance and wrong view is the most blameworthy thing.

140
00:11:48,623 --> 00:11:49,143
[SPEAKER_01]: Nothing else.

141
00:11:50,289 --> 00:11:55,253
[SPEAKER_00]: And also, that's why careless attention is in the beginning of the whole.

142
00:11:55,273 --> 00:11:56,174
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

143
00:11:56,575 --> 00:12:09,966
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why if somebody, somebody just sort of stops gradually, fades away, the assuming of that external dimension, he basically stops assuming the Atavāda.

144
00:12:10,327 --> 00:12:11,508
[SPEAKER_01]: That's exactly what Atavāda is.

145
00:12:12,589 --> 00:12:14,991
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he becomes free from the factor of the self-view.

146
00:12:16,893 --> 00:12:17,113
[SPEAKER_00]: So,

147
00:12:18,858 --> 00:12:25,502
[SPEAKER_00]: So, is it so that yoniso manasikāra is actually in regards to these things we are talking about?

148
00:12:25,722 --> 00:12:28,604
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, yoniso manasikāra... yoniso means womb.

149
00:12:29,124 --> 00:12:31,125
[SPEAKER_01]: So, basically, the root, the beginning.

150
00:12:32,075 --> 00:12:43,564
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you could see, like, if the normal attention is the attention that's usually completely focused upon the object, and even if it steps away from the object into a more general picture, it makes that an object.

151
00:12:44,005 --> 00:12:48,428
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it always... the measure for ordinary yoniso manasikāra is the object.

152
00:12:48,869 --> 00:12:49,269
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

153
00:12:49,329 --> 00:12:53,853
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't see anything simultaneously present that's peripheral to that attended object.

154
00:12:54,782 --> 00:13:18,651
[SPEAKER_01]: But if it starts to train itself to start noticing other things while it is still attending the present object, whatever that object is, whatever sense that might have been sort of most dominant, if it starts noticing those particular without making them into a new object, so you keep your old attention, so to speak, on the object, and simultaneously, while that is present, you start noticing other things that are there, simultaneously present, but not the object.

155
00:13:19,592 --> 00:13:27,318
[SPEAKER_01]: So, basically, you start training the mind to discern other things different from that which you're used to discerning, which is only an object.

156
00:13:27,338 --> 00:13:27,458
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

157
00:13:27,718 --> 00:13:28,399
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's the same.

158
00:13:28,559 --> 00:13:32,562
[SPEAKER_01]: Through the same attention, you start looking at the origins of that object.

159
00:13:32,622 --> 00:13:33,403
[SPEAKER_01]: What's behind it?

160
00:13:33,483 --> 00:13:34,224
[SPEAKER_01]: What's around it?

161
00:13:34,324 --> 00:13:35,164
[SPEAKER_00]: What's higher than it?

162
00:13:35,425 --> 00:13:36,586
[SPEAKER_00]: The nature of the structure.

163
00:13:36,606 --> 00:13:36,866
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

164
00:13:36,886 --> 00:13:38,587
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you start looking at the structure of it.

165
00:13:38,607 --> 00:13:41,309
[SPEAKER_01]: You start looking at the womb from which it all originated.

166
00:13:41,349 --> 00:13:42,971
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why it's yoniso, manasikāra.

167
00:13:42,991 --> 00:13:44,172
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a proper attention.

168
00:13:44,852 --> 00:13:56,616
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not like... people often translate proper attention improperly, or rather lazily and carelessly, whereby it just means like a... oh, it's a wholesome attention, just when you attend good and you think good.

169
00:13:56,636 --> 00:13:56,897
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

170
00:13:57,117 --> 00:13:59,257
[SPEAKER_01]: It's much more precise than that.

171
00:13:59,317 --> 00:14:00,818
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it's such a fundamental aspect.

172
00:14:00,878 --> 00:14:03,699
[SPEAKER_01]: It's one of the factors for the right view.

173
00:14:04,659 --> 00:14:06,620
[SPEAKER_01]: Equally important as the word of the Buddha.

174
00:14:06,680 --> 00:14:09,241
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can have all the words of the Buddha and all the Buddhas.

175
00:14:09,281 --> 00:14:13,563
[SPEAKER_01]: If you have no proper attention, none of that will make sense or take root.

176
00:14:14,583 --> 00:14:22,306
[SPEAKER_01]: So, basically, it's discerning the structure through the arisen thing, without sort of making it into a new thing, because then the structure is somewhere else.

177
00:14:22,846 --> 00:14:23,066
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

178
00:14:23,806 --> 00:14:27,528
[SPEAKER_01]: So, basically, that's how you train your attention to become proper attention.

179
00:14:27,548 --> 00:14:30,549
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it attends to whatever attends, but it sees beyond it.

180
00:14:30,949 --> 00:14:31,109
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

181
00:14:31,269 --> 00:14:34,530
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it doesn't get locked onto just the face value of the arisen thing.

182
00:14:34,610 --> 00:14:34,710
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

183
00:14:34,970 --> 00:14:35,250
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

184
00:14:35,710 --> 00:14:39,812
[SPEAKER_00]: I still feel that there is actually a danger that instead of, eh...

185
00:14:40,885 --> 00:14:48,989
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, seeing the structure, you just step back and you only broaden... Well, it has to start like that.

186
00:14:49,069 --> 00:14:50,530
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, it has to start like that.

187
00:14:50,550 --> 00:14:56,393
[SPEAKER_01]: But as long as you know that you'll be supposed to be seeing the peripheral in regard to whatever, it starts in the present.

188
00:14:56,413 --> 00:15:00,135
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yes, you step back, you broaden the experience, but then that becomes particular.

189
00:15:00,335 --> 00:15:00,816
[SPEAKER_01]: Isn't it?

190
00:15:01,076 --> 00:15:03,677
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, through that, you keep discerning further.

191
00:15:03,757 --> 00:15:06,899
[SPEAKER_01]: But if there is no end to it, it's infinite, in that sense.

192
00:15:07,439 --> 00:15:09,060
[SPEAKER_01]: You can keep doing that as much as you like.

193
00:15:10,362 --> 00:15:13,424
[SPEAKER_01]: And the whole point of doing it is to start discerning that principle.

194
00:15:13,944 --> 00:15:18,586
[SPEAKER_01]: Field and the thing that isn't upon the field, that defines field in its return.

195
00:15:19,047 --> 00:15:28,932
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's why, in some sense, you don't necessarily have to have kind of greatly developed mind to discern the principle of pratītyasamupāda, of that interdependence.

196
00:15:28,992 --> 00:15:32,714
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't need to, because you can discern the principle upon very basic things.

197
00:15:33,455 --> 00:15:34,997
[SPEAKER_01]: But you discern the same principle.

198
00:15:35,258 --> 00:15:35,438
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

199
00:15:35,458 --> 00:15:37,181
[SPEAKER_00]: There is this, there is that.

200
00:15:37,201 --> 00:15:37,421
[SPEAKER_00]: This.

201
00:15:37,762 --> 00:15:37,982
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

202
00:15:38,182 --> 00:15:38,403
[SPEAKER_01]: This.

203
00:15:38,663 --> 00:15:39,645
[SPEAKER_01]: With this, this is.

204
00:15:39,825 --> 00:15:40,066
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

205
00:15:40,546 --> 00:15:42,269
[SPEAKER_01]: When this is present, this is present.

206
00:15:42,289 --> 00:15:42,750
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

207
00:15:43,952 --> 00:15:45,455
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when there is a field...

208
00:15:47,113 --> 00:15:56,080
[SPEAKER_01]: For things to be sort of arisen upon, things arise upon it, which in return define that field as that which they're not at the time they have arisen upon it.

209
00:15:56,560 --> 00:16:02,084
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's fully mutually determining each other, and you realize that's how everything works in that sense.

210
00:16:02,305 --> 00:16:12,853
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yes, in a way, Bhaktisiddhānta Mahāprabhu explains how everything works in a very direct, insightful manner, whereby if you understand it, you automatically stop regarding things as yours.

211
00:16:13,493 --> 00:16:15,334
[SPEAKER_01]: You're automatically free from yourself.

212
00:16:16,275 --> 00:16:19,458
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're... basically, if you... if you fully develop it, you become an arahant.

213
00:16:19,838 --> 00:16:24,521
[SPEAKER_01]: But it doesn't explain it in a sense of telling you what's happening, and it provides as an explanation.

214
00:16:25,502 --> 00:16:36,150
[SPEAKER_01]: It kind of requires time, and it's sort of linear, and succession of things, and when this changes, and this goes, and then... and then, oh, yeah, they really... that's really... they really... they really make sense, or something like that.

215
00:16:36,490 --> 00:16:38,232
[SPEAKER_01]: It's much more precise than that.

216
00:16:39,709 --> 00:16:40,249
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you...

217
00:16:40,309 --> 00:16:41,730
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, it's not necessarily wrong.

218
00:16:41,850 --> 00:16:42,771
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, it is, actually.

219
00:16:42,811 --> 00:16:51,076
[SPEAKER_01]: It is wrong in a sense that... but this, somehow, should be taken as a precise thing, because then people will take it seriously, and... and then try to discern this very thing.

220
00:16:51,496 --> 00:16:54,798
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's very, very hard to discern, as you can see for yourself now.

221
00:16:54,838 --> 00:16:56,820
[SPEAKER_01]: You're starting to get the idea what it refers to.

222
00:16:57,420 --> 00:17:01,923
[SPEAKER_01]: But actually discerning it, and then discerning this relationship, it's just the same principle.

223
00:17:02,224 --> 00:17:12,391
[SPEAKER_01]: Because everybody, somebody starts with this, this is, and then with what, what is, and then it carries on into twelve factors, or sixteen factors, or eight factors, depending how the Buddha describes it on different occasions.

224
00:17:14,833 --> 00:17:17,515
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's the same, it's the same principle that's being described.

225
00:17:18,696 --> 00:17:25,822
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you could see what kind of understanding would be required to see that principle in all those intricate

226
00:17:27,783 --> 00:17:33,567
[SPEAKER_01]: ways of relating to each other, as the Buddha would have described, that it's a part of the birth and everything else, as phenomena.

227
00:17:34,067 --> 00:17:35,769
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, the good news is, you don't have to.

228
00:17:36,810 --> 00:17:38,070
[SPEAKER_01]: All you have to do is see the principle.

229
00:17:38,271 --> 00:17:38,471
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

230
00:17:38,551 --> 00:17:43,735
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is basically nothing else than saying, oh, yes, whatever has arisen, for that reason, it's impermanent.

231
00:17:44,255 --> 00:17:50,259
[SPEAKER_00]: Say, I understand, basically, if you really see the principle, then you abolish or extinguish that notion of right.

232
00:17:50,980 --> 00:17:51,180
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

233
00:17:51,240 --> 00:17:52,781
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the point.

234
00:17:52,821 --> 00:17:53,162
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

235
00:17:53,202 --> 00:17:56,244
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it was said in the suttas, Sariputra said,

236
00:17:57,932 --> 00:18:00,813
[SPEAKER_01]: I think, or the Buddha, the one who sees the... Sariputra.

237
00:18:00,833 --> 00:18:03,093
[SPEAKER_01]: Sariputra said, the one who sees dhamma, sees paticca upādāna.

238
00:18:03,253 --> 00:18:03,433
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

239
00:18:03,453 --> 00:18:06,153
[SPEAKER_01]: The one who sees paticca upādāna, sees the dhamma.

240
00:18:06,173 --> 00:18:06,814
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

241
00:18:08,974 --> 00:18:18,536
[SPEAKER_01]: And you realize, also, it must be as important as any other, kind of, directly relevant aspect of the dhamma.

242
00:18:21,316 --> 00:18:26,817
[SPEAKER_01]: But the problem is, there's so much, obviously, wrong notions, explanations, views, based upon paticca upādāna today.

243
00:18:26,877 --> 00:18:27,057
[SPEAKER_01]: So,

244
00:18:28,202 --> 00:18:31,203
[SPEAKER_01]: It's probably even better if people don't bother with it, in that sense.

245
00:18:31,543 --> 00:18:40,187
[SPEAKER_01]: Try to figure it out, because it's... because there's so much out, it's quite likely they'll be pulled to one wrong side or the other and end up doing it wrongly.

246
00:18:40,927 --> 00:18:44,029
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not... it's not necessarily crucial or a deal-breaker.

247
00:18:44,469 --> 00:18:52,712
[SPEAKER_01]: If a person still remains authentic and knows why they're practicing and what... what's supposed to sort of be uprooted with the understanding, they'll... they'll figure it out eventually.

248
00:18:53,133 --> 00:18:53,433
[SPEAKER_01]: But...

249
00:18:54,550 --> 00:18:55,771
[SPEAKER_01]: It's really not necessary.

250
00:18:55,851 --> 00:18:56,951
[SPEAKER_01]: Just understand the principle.

251
00:18:57,051 --> 00:19:00,913
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't worry about the twelve, or eight, or sixteen, whichever way the world is, probably, don't worry about it.

252
00:19:01,293 --> 00:19:03,214
[SPEAKER_01]: Just understand the two sticks supporting each other.

253
00:19:03,835 --> 00:19:06,116
[SPEAKER_01]: Just understand peripheral and actual, actual peripheral.

254
00:19:06,476 --> 00:19:06,656
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

255
00:19:07,336 --> 00:19:10,798
[SPEAKER_01]: Because fully understanding it will leave no room for the sense of self to be assumed at.

256
00:19:12,023 --> 00:19:12,423
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's all.

257
00:19:12,563 --> 00:19:14,205
[SPEAKER_01]: The self was never there, as you can see.

258
00:19:14,946 --> 00:19:16,507
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, by not knowing that it's there...

259
00:19:16,527 --> 00:19:25,815
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the presence of not-knowledge, of non-knowledge, that self cannot be there, the presence of that phenomena places the self there to the extent of that phenomena.

260
00:19:26,396 --> 00:19:35,684
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if your non-knowledge that self cannot be there was great, quite sort of spread around your experience, your self is to the same extent spread about.

261
00:19:36,225 --> 00:19:37,666
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're fully responsible for it.

262
00:19:38,803 --> 00:19:47,368
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, consequently, fully understood, fully removing the non-knowledge, replacing with knowledge that self cannot be anywhere there, the self is not anywhere there.

263
00:19:48,588 --> 00:19:52,630
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why there is the element of nibbāna in Putujana's experience.

264
00:19:53,571 --> 00:19:58,894
[SPEAKER_01]: It's nothing other than saying there's a possibility of him to achieve nibbāna, to free himself from himself.

265
00:19:59,583 --> 00:20:05,810
[SPEAKER_01]: And that is possible because the structure can never be actually penetrated or affected by the self.

266
00:20:06,271 --> 00:20:08,954
[SPEAKER_01]: If it were, that means the self would have some input.

267
00:20:09,895 --> 00:20:10,816
[SPEAKER_01]: And it would be.

268
00:20:10,876 --> 00:20:13,560
[SPEAKER_01]: It would be a sort of a master.

269
00:20:13,600 --> 00:20:14,100
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's not.

270
00:20:15,121 --> 00:20:16,743
[SPEAKER_01]: But until you know that, then it is.

271
00:20:19,323 --> 00:20:22,804
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it was said upādāna is not the five aggregates.

272
00:20:23,785 --> 00:20:27,146
[SPEAKER_01]: Not all of them, not even one of them, not even the bits of them, nothing.

273
00:20:27,686 --> 00:20:29,647
[SPEAKER_01]: But there is no upādāna without them.

274
00:20:30,527 --> 00:20:31,828
[SPEAKER_01]: So it's in regard to them.

275
00:20:33,080 --> 00:20:39,067
[SPEAKER_01]: In the same self, in the same sense, the self is in regard to the experience as a whole, but it's not actually there.

276
00:20:39,287 --> 00:20:43,932
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is why, for Pratyujana, who doesn't question that self, he assumes it elsewhere, i.e.

277
00:20:44,413 --> 00:20:45,694
[SPEAKER_01]: external to the whole thing.

278
00:20:46,255 --> 00:20:50,560
[SPEAKER_01]: Once you take that away, then he realizes, well, it cannot be elsewhere, so it can't hide there.

279
00:20:50,980 --> 00:20:52,262
[SPEAKER_01]: Certainly, it can't fit in here.

280
00:20:53,347 --> 00:20:53,888
[SPEAKER_01]: Then it fades.

281
00:20:54,348 --> 00:20:55,389
[SPEAKER_01]: You're left with no choice.

282
00:20:55,409 --> 00:21:03,055
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's why you kind of indirectly, you keep shutting down the escape, the escape routes for the assumption of the self.

283
00:21:03,075 --> 00:21:04,737
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't directly stop assuming.

284
00:21:05,377 --> 00:21:08,680
[SPEAKER_01]: That's when you know, wait a minute, the vision needs to be unscrewed.

285
00:21:09,461 --> 00:21:10,982
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't just knock it out as a trick.

